{"id":"oerlikon-20-mm-cannon","slug":"oerlikon-20-mm-cannon","name":"Oerlikon 20 mm cannon","aliases":["20 mm gun","Oerlikon F","Oerlikon L","Oerlikon S","Oerlikon 20","Oerlikon SS","Oerlikon gun","Oerlikon GAI","Oerlikon KAA","20mm Oerlikon","Oerlikon 20mm","20 mm Oerlikon","Oerlikon 20 mm","20 ITK Oerlikon","Oerlikon cannon","Oerlikon 20 mm gun","Oerlikon 20mm cannon","20 mm Oerlikon cannon","20mm antiaircraft gun","Oerlikon 20 mm cannons","Oerlikon 20mm autocannon","20mm Oerlikon (autocannon)","Oerlikon 20 mm (1 in) cannon"],"kind":"ordnance","category":"naval-guns","subcategory":"Series of autocannons","description":"The Oerlikon 20 mm cannon is a series of autocannons based on an original German Becker Type M2 20 mm cannon design that appeared very early in World War I. Widely produced by Oerlikon Contraves and by others, its various models saw service with both Allied and Axis forces during World War II. Versions of the cannon were still in use in 2026.","history":"During World War I, German industrialist Reinhold Becker developed a 20 mm caliber cannon, known now as the 20 mm Becker using the advanced primer ignition blowback (API blowback) method of operation. This used a 20×70mmRB cartridge and had a cyclic rate of fire of 300 rpm. It was used on a limited scale as an aircraft gun on Luftstreitkräfte warplanes, and an anti-aircraft gun towards the end of that war.\nBecause the Treaty of Versailles banned further production of such weapons in Germany, the patents and design works were transferred in 1919 to the Swiss firm SEMAG (Seebach Maschinenbau Aktien Gesellschaft) based near Zürich. SEMAG continued development of the weapon, and in 1924 had produced the SEMAG L, a heavier weapon (43 kg) that fired more powerful 20×100mmRB ammunition at a slightly higher rate of fire, 350 rpm.\nIn 1924, SEMAG failed. The Oerlikon firm, named after the Zürich suburb of Oerlikon where it was based, then acquired all rights to the weapon, plus the manufacturing equipment and the employees of SEMAG.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge (L70)","value":"20×110mmRB"},{"label":"Cartridge (L85)","value":"20×128mm"},{"label":"Action","value":"API blowback"},{"label":"Action","value":"Gas operated, power reserve loading"},{"label":"Rate of Fire (Cyclic)","value":"450 rounds per minute"},{"label":"Rate of Fire (Practical)","value":"250-320 rounds per minute"},{"label":"Rate of Fire (Cyclic)","value":"900 to 1,000 rounds per minute"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity (L70)","value":"820 m/s","numeric":820,"unit":"m/s"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity (L85)","value":"1050 m/s","numeric":1050,"unit":"m/s"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"Cylindrical magazine holding 60 rounds; later adjusted for belt-fed gun"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"L70"},{"label":"Barrel Length (Overall)","value":"2210 mm","numeric":2210,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Barrel Length (Barrel length)","value":"1400 mm","numeric":1400,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Barrel Length","value":"L85"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Effective Range","value":"Against low-flying aircraft (HE round)"},{"label":"Effective Range (L70)","value":"914 m","numeric":914,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Effective Range (L85)","value":"1500 m","numeric":1500,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Maximum Range","value":"HE round at 45°"},{"label":"Maximum Range (L70)","value":"4389 m","numeric":4389,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Maximum Range (L85)","value":"6800 m","numeric":6800,"unit":"m"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"Reinhold Becker"},{"label":"Designed","value":"1935"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Oerlikon"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Oerlikon Contraves"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1937−1995"},{"label":"Number Built","value":"124,734"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Switzerland"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1937–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":"20×110mmRB","Action":"API blowb